From the day-20 state: 2316 cumulative infections and 1944 currently infectious implies ~372 have recovered by day 20. With no travel/interventions until day 21, we continue the SIR progression with stochastic spread. On day 21 a vaccination campaign hits 25% coverage with 95% efficacy, effectively moving the susceptible fraction down by about \(0.25\times 0.95=0.2375\) of the population’s risk—so fewer new infections after day 21, and the epidemic should start tapering faster than it would otherwise. Given the SIR dynamics in a 5,000 population (peak around when susceptible fraction drops; here vaccination further lowers it), the additional cumulative infections from days 21–60 should be substantial but far from infecting everyone—most likely landing somewhere around the low-to-mid thousands overall, i.e., an additional ~1.0–1.7k infections beyond the 2316 by day 20. The remaining final-size uncertainty comes mainly from stochasticity in early post-day-21 transmission and the timing of susceptibles exhaustion.

