From day 0 to 20, cumulative infections rose to 1129 with 911 currently infectious, so the epidemic is still in its main growth phase. A vaccination campaign begins on day 21, reducing infection risk by 95% for 25% coverage, i.e. an effective susceptible fraction reduction factor of about \((1-0.25\times0.95)=0.7625\). This substantially reduces the remaining susceptible pool and should slow and eventually curb growth after day 21, but it won’t prevent many new infections before the epidemic response fully “works through” the current infectious cohorts.

Given the stochastic agent-based nature and that day-20 infectious count (911) implies strong ongoing transmission, a reasonable calibrated extrapolation is: by day 40 the additional cumulative infections will be on the order of a few hundred to around ~1500, with substantial spread. The corresponding distribution below reflects (i) continued growth for ~1–2 weeks after day 20, (ii) slowing/partial saturation due to vaccination, and (iii) stochastic variability.

