Reasoning (brief):
At end of day 20 Riverton has 2316 total infections (1944 currently infectious) and 2684 susceptible. On day 21, 25% of the population is vaccinated. Among susceptibles, about 25% would be vaccinated (roughly 671 individuals), leaving ~2013 unvaccinated susceptibles. Vaccinated susceptibles have 5% of the baseline infection probability per exposure (i.e., 0.0025 per contact vs 0.05 for unvaccinated). With about 6 contacts per day and infectious individuals averaging 10 days of infectiousness, the effective per-infectious infection rate is reduced but still substantial. This yields a remaining susceptible pool capable of supporting roughly 2.0–2.7k additional infections over the 40 days to day 60, with stochastic variation. A plausible forecast distribution (assuming random mixing and the stated vaccination effect) gives the following percentiles for new infections between day 20 end and day 60 end (inclusive): p10 ≈ 2000, p25 ≈ 2170, p50 ≈ 2350, p75 ≈ 2520, p90 ≈ 2670.

